{
  "title": "Engineering Long-Horizon Agents Beyond the Two-Hour Wall",
  "thesis": "Autonomous agents degrade over extended horizons not due to context window limits, but from compounding state drift and unvalidated environmental side effects. Achieving multi-hour reliability requires replacing unbroken linear trajectories with deterministic checkpointing and isolated verification sub-agents.",
  "outline": [
    {
      "heading": "The Two-Hour Execution Wall",
      "bullets": [
        "Frame the core problem examined at the October 31, 2026 Build Day: the steep drop in agent success rates between 90 and 120 minutes of continuous runtime.",
        "Contrast synthetic benchmarks with persistent production tasks like cross-repo migrations, dependency refactors, and multi-step infrastructure setup.",
        "Set the baseline metrics observed when running modern frontier models in unconstrained terminal and browser loops."
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "Live Demos and Failure Modes",
      "bullets": [
        "Deconstruct the live demo task: an autonomous migration of a legacy service that derailed at minute 114 after repeated shell silent failures.",
        "Analyze the prototypes shipped during the hackathon, including containerized rollback harnesses and state-graph loggers.",
        "Identify the three most common structural failures: hallucinated invariants, unpruned tool output bloat, and infinite recovery loops."
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "Why Belief States Diverge",
      "bullets": [
        "Synthesize the panel discussion on why large context windows exacerbate attention distraction rather than preserving semantic continuity.",
        "Explain how minor discrepancies in early tool call returns compound into invalid intermediate world models.",
        "Demonstrate why model self-reporting fails to diagnose drift once the underlying working memory contains conflicting assertions."
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "Deterministic Checkpoints and Rollouts",
      "bullets": [
        "Document the architectural pattern that proved effective: periodic snapshotting of file systems and external API states into deterministic checkpoints.",
        "Outline the out-of-band verifier agent pattern, which grades progress against hard assertions before committing context to main memory.",
        "Compare system latencies and token economics between continuous linear generation and branch-and-prune state machines."
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "What Builders Should Ship Next",
      "bullets": [
        "Define concrete interface patterns engineers should implement now: git-backed environment forks, structured error traps, and task heartbeats.",
        "Highlight open-source harnesses and evaluation suites targeting continuous 4-hour to 12-hour autonomous benchmarks.",
        "Direct builders to the demo repository, recorded panel sessions, and the next AGI House systems working group."
      ]
    }
  ]
}
